## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the integration of fundamental data structures—arrays, linked lists, hashmaps, and heaps—to solve a streaming scenario with sliding window deduplication and real-time ranking.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window deduplication per (user_id, action) using a hashmap with timestamp tracking
- Per-user score aggregation maintained in a hashmap for O(1) updates
- Global top-K ranking via max-heap with O(log K) insertion/deletion and lazy invalidation
- Efficient expiration handling using linked lists and amortized cleanup strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
class EventProcessor {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.latestByUserAction = new Map(); // (user_id,action) -> timestamp
    this.userTotals = new Map(); // user_id -> total_score
    this.userEvents = new Map(); // user_id -> linked list of (timestamp, score)
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap(); // (total_score, user_id)
  }

  addEvent(event) {
    const key = `${event.user_id}:${event.action}`;
    const latest = this.latestByUserAction.get(key);

    if (!latest || event.ts - latest > this.W) {
      // Update dedup map
      this.latestByUserAction.set(key, event.ts);

      // Update user total
      const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(event.user_id) || 0;
      const newTotal = currentTotal + event.score;
      this.userTotals.set(event.user_id, newTotal);

      // Add to user's event list
      if (!this.userEvents.has(event.user_id)) {
        this.userEvents.set(event.user_id, new LinkedList());
      }
      this.userEvents.get(event.user_id).push({ts: event.ts, score: event.score});

      // Update heap
      this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: event.user_id});
    }
  }

  getTopK(k, now) {
    this.expire(now);
    return this.maxHeap.getTopK(k);
  }

  expire(now) {
    for (const [userId, events] of this.userEvents) {
      while (events.length > 0 && now - events.head.ts > this.W) {
        const expired = events.shift();
        const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId);
        const newTotal = currentTotal - expired.score;
        this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);

        if (newTotal > 0) {
          this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: userId});
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
**Time:** O(1) amortized per event for updates, O(log K) for top-K retrieval
**Space:** O(N) where N is number of active users plus events within window W

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests advanced dynamic programming: partitioning with a non-trivial segment cost that depends on min and max, requiring careful state design and in-loop maintenance of subproblem results. It also validates producing both memoized and iterative solutions and analyzing time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- DP with partition states
- In-loop maintenance of running min and max
- Top-down memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Time complexity: O(D*N^2); Space: O(D*N)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Top-down memoized
function minCost(A, D){
  const N = A.length;
  const memo = Array.from({length:D+1}, ()=> Array(N).fill(null));
  function solve(d, i){
    if (d === 1){
      let mn = Infinity, mx = -Infinity;
      for (let k = 0; k <= i; k++){ mn = Math.min(mn, A[k]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[k]); }
      return mn * mx;
    }
    if (memo[d][i] != null) return memo[d][i];
    let mn = Infinity, mx = -Infinity; let best = Infinity;
    for (let j = i; j >= d-1; j--){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
      best = Math.min(best, solve(d-1, j-1) + mn*mx);
    }
    memo[d][i] = best; return best;
  }
  return solve(D, N-1);
}

// Bottom-up
function minCostBottom(A, D){
  const N = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:D+1}, ()=> Array(N).fill(INF));
  // base: d = 1
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++){
    let mn = A[i], mx = A[i];
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
      if (j === 0) dp[1][i] = Math.min(dp[1][i], mn*mx);
    }
  }
  for (let d = 2; d <= D; d++){
    for (let i = d-1; i < N; i++){
      let mn = A[i], mx = A[i];
      for (let j = i; j >= d-1; j--){
        mn = Math.min(mn, A[j]); mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
        dp[d][i] = Math.min(dp[d][i], dp[d-1][j-1] + mn*mx);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[D][N-1];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Understanding three-way partitioning shows depth beyond basic quicksort, especially for duplicates. It also tests in-place mutation and careful loop invariants.

## Key Concepts

- Three-way partitioning around a pivot
- In-place sorting with O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case time, handling duplicates

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(arr, left=0, right=arr.length-1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const pivot = arr[Math.floor((left + right) / 2)];
  let lt = left, i = left, gt = right;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(arr, left, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(arr, gt + 1, right);
}
```

